    fullDescription: "The Battistero di San Giovanni is the oldest building in the heart of Florence, an octagonal Romanesque baptistery clad in white and green marble that stands directly in front of the Duomo and was long believed by Florentines to be an ancient Roman temple — so revered that Dante was baptised here, as were generations of the city's children. Its fame rests on two glories. Outside are the great bronze doors, above all the east set facing the cathedral, which Michelangelo is said to have called the 'Gates of Paradise': ten gilded panels of Old Testament scenes that Lorenzo Ghiberti laboured over for 27 years and which, with their mastery of perspective and depth, effectively announced the arrival of Renaissance sculpture; the panels on the building are faithful copies, the restored originals now safely displayed in the nearby Opera del Duomo museum. Inside, the ceiling erupts into one of the most breathtaking sights in the city — a vast thirteenth-century mosaic of over a million gold tesserae depicting the Last Judgment, ranks of angels and biblical scenes, dominated by a colossal figure of Christ, all glowing overhead like the inside of a jewel box. The baptistery is included in the combined Duomo tickets (the Brunelleschi, Giotto or Ghiberti passes), so it makes natural sense to visit it alongside the dome climb, the campanile and the museum. It is small and a visit takes only twenty minutes or so, but standing beneath that golden vault is one of the unforgettable moments of Florence.",
