    fullDescription: "Giotto's Campanile is the Duomo's elegant companion, an 85-metre free-standing bell tower rising beside the cathedral in the same harmonious skin of white, green and pink marble, and one of the most beautiful Gothic towers in Italy. It was begun in 1334 by the painter Giotto, then the most celebrated artist in Florence and appointed master of the cathedral works, though he died three years later having completed only the lower level; the tower was carried on by Andrea Pisano and finished by Francesco Talenti in 1359. Its lower storeys are decorated with a remarkable series of relief panels and statues — the originals now in the Opera del Duomo museum, with copies in place — depicting the story of humanity, the planets, the virtues, the sacraments and the liberal arts, a whole medieval encyclopaedia carved in stone. For visitors the great draw is the climb: 414 steps up a series of landings to a terrace at the top. Many find it a more rewarding ascent than the dome itself, partly because the staircase is less cramped and has viewing platforms to pause on along the way, and above all because from here you look straight across at Brunelleschi's dome in all its glory — the one great view of Florence that the dome climb, being inside it, cannot offer. It is included in the combined Duomo passes, so plan it alongside the baptistery and museum, and choose either the campanile or the dome for your climb if you cannot face both sets of steps in one day.",
