    fullDescription: "San Lorenzo was the parish church of the Medici, and the complex around it charts the family's rise from merchants to grand dukes and their unrivalled patronage of the Renaissance. The basilica itself, rebuilt from 1419 by Filippo Brunelleschi, is a masterpiece of early-Renaissance restraint — cool grey pietra serena columns and arches laid out with mathematical harmony against plain white walls — though its façade was left as bare brown stone, a design by Michelangelo that was never executed. Inside are Donatello's dramatic bronze pulpits, his last works, and the Old Sacristy, another Brunelleschi jewel decorated by Donatello. Adjoining the church is the Laurentian Library, whose extraordinary vestibule and cascading stone staircase Michelangelo designed in a restless, almost proto-Baroque style that broke every classical rule. But the unmissable element, reached by a separate entrance and ticket at the back, is the Cappelle Medicee, the Medici Chapels: the opulent, dome-topped Chapel of the Princes lined in dark inlaid marble and semi-precious stone, and beyond it Michelangelo's New Sacristy, where he sculpted the brooding allegorical figures of Dawn, Dusk, Day and Night reclining on the tombs of two Medici dukes — among the most powerful funerary sculptures ever made. Around the church sprawls the leather-and-souvenir stalls of the San Lorenzo market and the food halls of the Mercato Centrale, so a visit combines art, history and lunch. Buy the chapels ticket separately, and do not leave without seeing Michelangelo's tombs.",
