    fullDescription: "The Museo Nazionale del Bargello is Florence's finest sculpture collection and, because it is overshadowed by the David-focused Accademia and the painting-heavy Uffizi, one of its most rewarding and least crowded museums. It occupies a forbidding medieval palace begun in 1255, the oldest public building in the city, which served over the centuries as the seat of the chief magistrate, the police headquarters (the 'bargello') and a prison with a grim history of executions, its severe courtyard now hung with the carved coats of arms of former magistrates. Inside, the collection is a roll-call of Renaissance sculpture. The star is Donatello's bronze David of the 1440s, the first free-standing nude cast since antiquity and a work of startling, ambiguous sensuality that helped define the era; his earlier marble David and the swaggering St George stand nearby. Around them are early works by Michelangelo, including his Bacchus and a tondo of the Madonna; Giambologna's famous winged Mercury poised on a breath of air; and the two competing trial panels for the Baptistery doors by Ghiberti and Brunelleschi, displayed side by side so you can judge the contest that launched the Renaissance. Further rooms hold Della Robbia glazed terracottas, medieval ivories, arms and armour and decorative arts. It is a manageable, uncrowded museum that rewards a couple of hours, and for anyone who wants to understand Florentine sculpture beyond the one giant marble in the Accademia, it is essential — check the sometimes-restricted opening hours before you go.",
