    fullDescription: "Santa Maria Novella is the principal Dominican church of Florence and the first great basilica most visitors pass, since it stands right beside the main railway station that shares its name. Its façade is one of the landmarks of Renaissance architecture: the lower Gothic section of green and white marble was topped in the 1460s by Leon Battista Alberti with a serenely classical upper storey and the elegant scrolled volumes that reconciled the two, a design of mathematical harmony that influenced church fronts for centuries. The vast, austere Gothic interior is a gallery of masterpieces. Its greatest treasure is Masaccio's fresco of the Holy Trinity, painted around 1427 and generally regarded as the first painting to deploy true, mathematically constructed linear perspective, opening an illusionistic chapel into the wall so convincingly that contemporaries were astonished. Beyond it are Domenico Ghirlandaio's radiant fresco cycle in the choir chapel (in which a young Michelangelo may have trained), Filippino Lippi's frescoes, Giotto's Crucifix hanging in the nave, and Brunelleschi's wooden Crucifix in a side chapel. The tranquil green cloisters alongside, frescoed by Paolo Uccello, and a small museum complete the visit. A short walk away is the Officina Profumo-Farmaceutica di Santa Maria Novella, an ancient monastic pharmacy and perfumery founded by the friars in 1221 and still selling soaps, scents and elixirs in frescoed rooms — a fragrant, atmospheric stop worth combining with the church. Allow around an hour for the basilica and cloisters.",
