    fullDescription: "The Mercato Centrale is the belly of Florence, a magnificent nineteenth-century market hall of cast iron and glass built in 1874, and it offers one of the best and most affordable food experiences in the city across two very different floors. The ground floor is the traditional produce market, a bustling, aromatic labyrinth of stalls where Florentines and chefs still shop for the raw materials of Tuscan cooking: butchers with hanging bistecca and trays of tripe, cheesemongers stacked with pecorino, delis heaped with prosciutto, salami and fresh pasta, fishmongers, greengrocers piled with seasonal vegetables, porcini and truffles, and bakers and wine sellers. It is a wonderful place to browse, photograph, taste and pick up picnic supplies or edible souvenirs. Upstairs, a soaring space was transformed in 2014 into a modern gourmet food hall, open late into the evening, where a ring of specialist counters cook everything to order — fresh pasta, pizza from a wood oven, steak, fried seafood, cured meats, gelato, craft beer and Tuscan wine — which you carry to communal tables in the buzzy central seating area. It is casual, lively and reasonably priced, a great option for a flexible lunch or dinner where everyone in a group can eat what they fancy. Don't miss the chance to try Florentine street-food classics like a lampredotto or bollito panino from a traditional stall. Surrounded by the leather and souvenir stalls of the San Lorenzo market, it combines easily with the basilica and Medici Chapels next door.",
