    fullDescription: "San Miniato al Monte is the loveliest church in Florence and, for many visitors, the most memorable single place in the city — a perfect Romanesque basilica crowning the highest point on the south bank, a short climb above the crowds of Piazzale Michelangelo yet blessedly peaceful. Begun in 1013 on the spot where, legend holds, the beheaded early-Christian martyr St Minias carried his own severed head, it presents one of the most beautiful façades in Tuscany: a geometric composition of green Prato and white Carrara marble, crowned by a glittering thirteenth-century mosaic of Christ between the Virgin and St Minias and topped by a gilded eagle, the emblem of the wool guild that funded it. The interior is equally rare and atmospheric, little changed in a thousand years: a raised choir above an ancient crypt, an intricate inlaid marble pavement patterned with zodiac signs and animals, more golden apse mosaics, a free-standing Renaissance chapel by Michelozzo and a sacristy frescoed with the life of St Benedict by Spinello Aretino. A community of Benedictine (Olivetan) monks still lives here, and if you time your visit for late afternoon you can hear them sing Gregorian chant at vespers in the candlelit crypt — an unforgettable experience. Outside, the terrace and the monumental cemetery beside the church offer a view over Florence that rivals or beats the famous one just below, and with far fewer people. Combine it with sunset at the piazzale, but save your real time for the church at the top — it is free, and it is the soul of the city.",
