    fullDescription: "The Corridoio Vasariano is one of the most extraordinary and romantic structures in Florence — a kilometre-long covered passageway that Giorgio Vasari built in just five months in 1565 on the orders of Cosimo I de' Medici, so that the ruling family could walk in privacy and safety between their offices at the Palazzo Vecchio and Uffizi and their new residence across the river at the Palazzo Pitti, without ever setting foot among the citizens below. Threading through buildings, along the top of the Ponte Vecchio — which is why the bridge's malodorous butchers were replaced with goldsmiths — and skirting a church, the corridor is a physical expression of Medici power and paranoia, and it later housed the gallery's celebrated collection of artists' self-portraits. After being closed to the public for decades, and shut entirely following a 1993 Mafia bombing nearby, the corridor underwent a major restoration and reopened in 2024, giving visitors the rare chance to retrace the grand-ducal route and enjoy its unique views out over the Arno and the bridge through its windows. Access is strictly by pre-booked, timed ticket, generally combined with entry to the Uffizi, and numbers are limited, so it must be reserved well in advance. It is a specialist experience rather than a first-timer's essential — the newly reopened corridor is now presented more as an architectural and historical walkway than the dense picture gallery it once was — but for repeat visitors, Medici obsessives and anyone fascinated by the secret machinery of Renaissance power, walking Vasari's hidden passage is a genuine thrill.",
