    fullDescription: "Cibrèo is one of the most storied names in Florentine dining, the creation of the late chef Fabio Picchi, who built a small culinary empire around the Sant'Ambrogio market — an elegant formal restaurant, a more casual and cheaper trattoria (the Cibrèino), a café and a theatre-kitchen — all dedicated to a passionate, uncompromising vision of traditional Tuscan cooking. Its most famous quirk is that the kitchen serves no pasta at all, a deliberate rejection of the dish Picchi felt had come to dominate Italian menus, focusing instead on the deeper roots of Florentine cucina: rich soups and the signature yellow pepper soup, flans and sformati, offal dishes prepared with real artistry, slow-braised and roasted meats, and superb seasonal vegetables, all sourced largely from the market next door. There are no printed menus in the traditional sense either; a waiter recites the day's dishes and guides you through them, and meals unfold at an unhurried, ceremonious pace. The formal ristorante is a special-occasion experience with prices to match and is best booked ahead, while the adjoining trattoria offers many of the same flavours in a simpler, cheaper setting with shared tables and no reservations — an excellent way to sample the Cibrèo philosophy without the full outlay. Following Picchi's death the family has continued the tradition. For serious eaters who want to understand authentic Tuscan cooking beyond the tourist staples, and who don't mind a kitchen that does things emphatically its own way, Cibrèo is a landmark worth seeking out east of the centre.",
