    fullDescription: "Piazza Santo Spirito is the living room of the Oltrarno and the beating heart of Florence's more local, bohemian nightlife, a handsome tree-shaded square presided over by the plain, unfinished façade of Brunelleschi's Santo Spirito church and ringed by an ever-changing cast of bars, cafés, trattorias and gelaterias. By day it hosts a morning market and a relaxed neighbourhood scene, but it comes into its own after dark, especially in the warmer months, when a young, mixed crowd of Florentines, students, artists and clued-up visitors gathers to drink, eat, talk and hang out, often perched on the wide steps of the church with drinks bought from the surrounding bars, giving the whole square the feel of an open-air party. It is the antithesis of a slick, expensive cocktail bar — the appeal lies in its easy, sociable, slightly scruffy authenticity, the sense of being where locals actually spend their evenings rather than in a tourist enclave. The bars around the edges range from simple spots pouring cheap spritzes and Tuscan wine to livelier venues with music, and there are excellent restaurants and aperitivo options among them for turning drinks into dinner. It can get boisterous and, being a genuine public square, has a rough-and-ready edge late at night, but it is generally good-natured. For a taste of how young Florence really socialises, an inexpensive drink under the trees and a warm, convivial atmosphere with none of the polish or price of the smart bars across the river, an evening on Piazza Santo Spirito is essential.",
