    fullDescription: "The Officina Profumo-Farmaceutica di Santa Maria Novella is one of the oldest pharmacies in the world and one of the most extraordinary shops anywhere, a place where buying a bar of soap becomes a genuine cultural experience. It traces its origins to 1221, when the Dominican friars who had settled at Santa Maria Novella began cultivating herbs and preparing medicinal remedies, balms, tinctures and distillates in their monastery; over the centuries this developed into a renowned apothecary, and it famously created a perfume, the Acqua della Regina, for Catherine de' Medici. Opened to the public in the seventeenth century and still operating today, it occupies a series of breathtaking historic rooms behind the church — a frescoed former chapel now serving as the main sales hall, vaulted antique salons lined with old apothecary jars, wooden cabinets and display cases, all evoking a hushed, museum-like atmosphere far removed from an ordinary store. It continues to produce and sell an exquisite range of perfumes, colognes, soaps, creams, pot-pourri, herbal elixirs and cosmetics based on the friars' old recipes and natural ingredients, beautifully packaged and making some of the most elegant and characterful gifts and souvenirs you can carry home from Florence. The products are premium but the range spans affordable soaps and scented waters up to fine perfumes, and — crucially — you are free to wander, browse, smell and simply admire the astonishing surroundings whether or not you buy. Combine a visit with the neighbouring basilica of Santa Maria Novella. For a shopping experience unlike any other, steeped in eight centuries of history, it is unmissable.",
