    fullDescription: "The Boboli Gardens are the perfect antidote to museum fatigue for families visiting Florence, a huge and beautiful sixteenth-century formal garden rising up the hillside behind the Pitti Palace where children can run, roam and let off steam after too many galleries and churches. Laid out for the Medici, it is one of the earliest and most influential Italian gardens, a designed landscape of long gravel avenues, clipped hedges, terraces, ornamental lakes and dozens of fountains and statues, and for kids it works brilliantly as a giant open-air treasure hunt: they can search for the dramatic Grotta del Buontalenti, an artificial cave dripping with fantastical sculpted figures; the amphitheatre with its Egyptian obelisk; the Neptune fountain; the island garden of the Isolotto; and statues large and comic hidden among the greenery, including the famous plump dwarf riding a tortoise. There is plenty of space to picnic, shade under the trees on hot days, and lovely views back over the city and the Duomo from the upper terraces. Practical notes for families: the garden is large and hilly with gravel paths, so it can be a workout with very young children or strollers, and there is limited food and water inside, so bring supplies; but the freedom and space it offers are exactly what children need in an intense sightseeing city. Combine it with the adjacent, quieter Bardini Garden, whose terrace has an even better view, and reward everyone with a gelato afterwards. For a relaxed, green half-day that keeps kids happy while adults enjoy the history and views, Boboli is ideal.",
