    fullDescription: "The Museo Galileo is one of the best museums in Florence for families and a brilliant, often-overlooked alternative for children who might struggle with the Renaissance paintings of the Uffizi next door. Dedicated to the history of science, it houses an extraordinary collection of scientific instruments assembled by the Medici and Lorraine grand dukes, and its star attractions have real wow-factor for kids and adults alike: two of Galileo Galilei's original telescopes, the very instruments with which he observed the moons of Jupiter and changed our understanding of the universe, along with — more macabrely fascinating to many children — one of his preserved fingers, displayed as a relic. Beyond the Galileo material, the beautifully presented galleries are full of things to marvel at: enormous antique globes and armillary spheres, elaborate astronomical clocks, early mechanical calculators, navigational and surveying instruments, telescopes and microscopes, and a memorable collection of eighteenth-century anatomical and obstetric wax and terracotta models used to teach medicine. Crucially for families, the museum includes interactive stations and clear, engaging explanations that let children experiment with scientific principles hands-on, making abstract ideas tangible and fun. It is a manageable size, so it does not overwhelm, and its riverside location right beside the Uffizi makes it easy to slot into a day's sightseeing, while its indoor setting makes it an excellent rainy-day or hot-afternoon option. For curious kids aged roughly six and up, budding scientists, and anyone wanting a change from art and churches, the Museo Galileo is engaging, educational and genuinely enjoyable — one of the smartest family choices in the city.",
