    fullDescription: "For families with older children, climbing to the top of the Duomo complex is one of the most exciting and rewarding things to do in Florence — a real physical adventure that turns sightseeing into a sense of achievement and rewards the effort with an unforgettable view over the terracotta rooftops of the city. There are two climbs to choose from, both included in the combined Duomo passes. The first is Brunelleschi's dome itself, a 463-step ascent that winds up the curving space between the dome's inner and outer shells, passing right beside Vasari's dramatic and slightly scary Last Judgment fresco of heaven and hell before emerging onto the lantern at the very top; it is a genuine thrill, but the staircase is steep, narrow, one-way and can feel claustrophobic, so it suits confident children of around seven and up who are comfortable in tight spaces, and requires a compulsory timed-entry slot booked ahead. The second, and often the better choice for families, is the climb up Giotto's Campanile, the bell tower beside the cathedral: at 414 steps it is a similar height, but the staircase is roomier, has several landings and terraces where you can stop, rest and take in the view on the way up, and is far less enclosed — and, as a bonus, from the top you get the perfect photograph of the great dome itself, which you cannot capture while inside it. Either way, wear good shoes, carry water, avoid the hottest part of the day, and be honest about young children's tolerance for stairs and heights. For energetic kids, reaching the top under their own steam is a highlight of the trip.",
