    fullDescription: "The Leonardo Interactive Museum is a compact, hands-on attraction dedicated to Leonardo da Vinci the inventor and engineer, and its touch-everything ethos makes it especially good for younger children who need to do rather than just look. The core of the collection is a set of full-scale wooden models of Leonardo's celebrated machines and mechanisms — flying contraptions and a giant 'aerial screw' helicopter, war machines, gears, cranes, hydraulic devices, bridges and mechanical studies — reconstructed from the drawings in his notebooks, and, crucially, many of them are designed to be handled, so kids can turn cranks, pull levers, spin wheels and set the models in motion to see the engineering principles at work for themselves. This interactivity keeps children engaged far better than a static gallery, and simple explanations (in multiple languages) connect the machines to Leonardo's restless genius. Alongside the inventions, the museum usually includes reproductions and interactive presentations exploring his art, including analyses of the Mona Lisa and his painting techniques, and some branches feature digital and immersive elements. It is a small museum that takes around an hour, making it an easy, affordable and low-commitment stop, and its central location and indoor setting make it a reliable rainy-day or hot-afternoon option to break up a day of churches and galleries. There are a few similar Leonardo- and Michelangelo-themed interactive museums dotted around the centre of Florence, all with a comparable hands-on approach. For families with children who love building, machines and how-things-work exhibits, and who benefit from being allowed to touch and play, it is an enjoyable and educational choice.",
