    fullDescription: "The Giardino Bardini is one of Florence's loveliest and most peaceful green spaces, a beautifully restored hillside garden in the Oltrarno that makes a calmer, less crowded alternative to the much larger Boboli Gardens next door and a delightful spot for a relaxed family break away from the busy streets. Climbing the slope below the Costa San Giorgio, the garden is a designed landscape of three distinct areas — a formal Italianate section with a grand Baroque staircase, an English-style wooded park and an agricultural stretch with orchards — laced with paths, fountains, statues and flowerbeds that change with the seasons. Its most celebrated feature is the wisteria pergola, a tunnel that erupts into a spectacular canopy of hanging purple blooms in April and May, drawing photographers and families alike, while at other times of year roses, irises, hydrangeas and fruit trees keep it colourful. For children there is space to wander and explore at an easy pace, gentle terraces rather than the long hilly hikes of Boboli, and plenty to discover among the statuary and water features. The real reward, though, is the view: the garden's upper terrace and its charming café command one of the finest panoramas over the whole of Florence — the dome, the towers, the river — arguably even better than the famous Piazzale Michelangelo, and far more tranquil, making it a perfect place to pause with a drink or an ice cream while children rest. Its ticket is often combined with Boboli, so the two can be visited together, and it links to the walk up toward San Miniato and Piazzale Michelangelo. For a serene, scenic and stroller-friendly green escape with a knockout view, the Bardini is a gem.",
