    fullDescription: "Pisa is the most popular and accessible day trip from Florence, an easy hour away by frequent regional train, and while it is often dismissed as a quick photo stop, the extraordinary ensemble of the Piazza dei Miracoli genuinely rewards a visit. This broad green square, a UNESCO World Heritage Site on the edge of the old city, gathers four dazzling white-marble Romanesque monuments together: the famous Leaning Tower, the cathedral's freestanding bell tower which began to tilt during construction in the twelfth century because of soft, unstable ground and now leans at a gravity-defying angle; the beautiful striped Duomo itself, one of the finest cathedrals in Italy; the vast, acoustically astonishing circular Baptistery; and the Camposanto, the monumental walled cemetery said to hold soil brought back from the Holy Land. You can climb the Leaning Tower via a booked, timed ticket for the novelty of ascending its tilting spiral staircase and the view from the top, though numbers are limited so it pays to reserve ahead. Beyond the famous square, Pisa is a real, lively university city with an attractive riverfront along the Arno, medieval streets and squares, and good, cheap student-oriented eating, so it is worth walking into the centre rather than only seeing the tower. Half a day is ample for most visitors: take a morning train, do the piazza and perhaps the tower climb and a wander into town, and be back in Florence by afternoon. It combines easily with nearby Lucca on a longer day out. For the iconic tower photo and a cluster of world-class monuments with minimal effort, Pisa is the obvious choice.",
