    fullDescription: "Siena is the great rival to Florence and, for many visitors, the most beautiful medieval city in Tuscany, a perfectly preserved hill town whose glory days as a banking and artistic power ended when plague and Florentine conquest froze it in time — to our lasting benefit. Its heart is the Piazza del Campo, one of the finest squares in Europe, a vast sloping shell-shaped space of warm brick that fans out from the soaring Palazzo Pubblico and its slender Torre del Mangia bell tower, and which twice each summer becomes the arena for the Palio, the thrilling bareback horse race between the city's ancient contrade (districts) that is the fierce, beating heart of Sienese identity. A short climb away rises the Duomo, one of the most spectacular cathedrals in Italy, a striped confection of black and white marble crammed with treasures: an intricate inlaid marble floor, a pulpit by Nicola Pisano, frescoes by Pinturicchio in the Piccolomini Library, and works by Donatello and Michelangelo. Beyond these headline sights, Siena rewards simply wandering its steep, atmospheric medieval lanes, exploring the Gothic churches and the Santa Maria della Scala museum, and sampling local specialities like panforte and pici pasta with a glass of Chianti. It deserves a full day: take the direct coach, which is faster than the train and drops you nearer the centre, arrive in the morning, and give yourself time to climb the tower, tour the cathedral and soak up the Campo over lunch before returning in the evening. Siena is the essential Tuscan companion to Florence, and one of the highlights of any trip to the region.",
