    fullDescription: "San Gimignano is one of the most striking sights in Tuscany, a tiny, perfectly preserved medieval walled town crowning a hill amid the vineyards and cypresses of the Val d'Elsa, and famous above all for its skyline of tall stone towers. In the Middle Ages the town's wealthy families competed to build ever-higher tower-houses as symbols of status and power, and at the peak there were some seventy of them; fourteen survive today, giving the small hilltop settlement an astonishing, almost modern silhouette that has earned it the nickname the 'Manhattan of the Middle Ages' and a place on the UNESCO World Heritage list. Within its walls, the town is a delight to explore on foot: interconnected piazzas paved in brick, the Collegiata church with vivid fresco cycles, the civic museum in the Palazzo Comunale, and the tallest tower, the Torre Grossa, which you can climb for a sweeping view over the terracotta roofs and the surrounding wine country. San Gimignano is also the home of Vernaccia, a crisp white wine that is one of Tuscany's finest, best enjoyed in a local enoteca, and the town's central square boasts a multi-award-winning gelateria that draws long queues. Because it is small and compact, half a day is enough to see the highlights, though its beauty and atmosphere reward lingering, especially once the day-trippers leave in the late afternoon. It has no direct rail link, so it is most easily reached by car or, very popularly, as part of an organised day tour from Florence that typically combines it with Siena and a Chianti vineyard, making an excellent full day out. For medieval atmosphere, unforgettable towers and superb wine, San Gimignano is a Tuscan gem.",
