    fullDescription: "The Chianti region, the swathe of gorgeous countryside rolling south from Florence toward Siena, is the archetypal Tuscan landscape — corrugated hills striped with vineyards and silvery olive groves, lines of dark cypress trees, honey-stone farmhouses and hilltop villages, all producing the famous Chianti Classico wine marked by its black-rooster seal. A day exploring it is one of the most rewarding and relaxing escapes from the city, a chance to swap art and crowds for wine, food and glorious scenery. The classic route is the SR222, the Chiantigiana road, which winds through the heart of the region linking a string of appealing wine towns: Greve in Chianti, with its funnel-shaped market square and famous butcher; nearby Panzano, home to the celebrity butcher Dario Cecchini; and the more refined, quieter villages of Radda and Castellina in Chianti, each with medieval centres, enoteche and sweeping views. Scattered through the hills are countless wineries and estates, from grand historic castles to small family producers, most of which welcome visitors by appointment for tours of the cellars and vineyards and, of course, tastings paired with local cheese, salami, olive oil and bread. Because tasting wine and driving do not mix, the best ways to experience Chianti are to join one of the many organised wine tours from Florence, which handle the driving and arrange winery visits and often a Tuscan lunch, or to hire a car with a designated driver. Either way, allow a full, unhurried day. For wine lovers, food lovers and anyone wanting to see the postcard Tuscan countryside up close, a day in Chianti is an absolute highlight of a Florence trip.",
