    fullDescription: "Lucca is one of the most charming and relaxed cities in Tuscany, and a delightful, less-hectic alternative to the region's busier stars, easily reached from Florence by regional train. Its defining feature is its walls: Lucca is completely encircled by a magnificent, fully intact ring of Renaissance ramparts, broad and tree-lined, which have been turned into an elevated linear park that loops right around the old city — and the quintessential Lucca experience is to hire a bicycle and cycle or stroll the entire four-kilometre circuit, looking down over the rooftops on one side and out to the surrounding countryside and mountains on the other. Within the walls, the compact historic centre is a joy to wander on foot or by bike, a maze of narrow medieval streets, handsome churches and lovely squares, the most remarkable of which is the Piazza dell'Anfiteatro, an oval ring of houses built directly on the foundations of a Roman amphitheatre, whose shape it perfectly preserves. Other highlights include the striped Romanesque façade of the Duomo di San Martino, the Guinigi Tower crowned improbably with holm-oak trees growing at its top (climbable for a view), and the birthplace of the composer Giacomo Puccini, celebrated with a festival and museum. Because it is less overrun than Pisa or Siena and has a genuine, lived-in local atmosphere, Lucca is a relaxing and rewarding place to spend a half or full day, ideal for a gentler, more leisurely taste of Tuscany. It combines very easily with Pisa, just twenty minutes away by train, for a full day seeing both. For cycling the walls, easy charm and an unhurried pace, Lucca is a lovely choice.",
