    fullDescription: "The Cinque Terre — the 'Five Lands' — are among the most photographed and beloved places in Italy, a string of five tiny, impossibly picturesque fishing villages clinging to the steep, terraced cliffs of the rugged Ligurian coast, their tightly packed houses painted in cheerful pastel shades of pink, yellow and ochre tumbling down to little harbours and the deep-blue Mediterranean. Monterosso, Vernazza, Corniglia, Manarola and Riomaggiore, all part of a protected national park and UNESCO World Heritage Site, are linked by a scenic coastal railway that threads through tunnels and pops out at each village, as well as by boats in season and by a network of famous, sometimes challenging hiking trails that wind along the clifftops and through the vineyard terraces, offering breathtaking sea views. From Florence it is a long day — roughly two and a half hours each way by train, usually changing at Pisa or Genoa for La Spezia and the local Cinque Terre line — so it is the most distant of the popular day trips and makes for a full, early-start-to-late-return outing, but the payoff is spectacular. The best approach is to buy a Cinque Terre Card, which covers unlimited local train journeys between the villages and access to the maintained walking trails, hop between two or three of the villages by train rather than trying to see all five, walk a stretch of coastal path if you are up for it, swim from Monterosso's beach or Vernazza's little harbour, and eat fresh seafood, pesto (a Ligurian invention) and fried anchovies with a glass of local white wine. Given the distance, many visitors prefer an organised tour to handle the logistics. It is a long haul, but for dramatic coastal scenery unlike anything else within reach of Florence, the Cinque Terre are unforgettable.",
