    fullDescription: "Summer in Florence is hot, humid and crowded, and it demands a smart strategy. The city sits in a bowl-shaped valley enclosed by hills, which traps the heat and makes it notably sultry: average highs sit around 30°C in July and August and regularly climb past 35°C during heatwaves, with strong sun, high humidity and little rain, and the stone city radiates warmth well into the evening. This is peak tourist season, so the major sights — above all the Uffizi, the Accademia and the Duomo dome — are at their most crowded and flight and hotel prices at their highest, even as the weather is arguably the least comfortable for sightseeing. The way to enjoy it is to adopt an early-and-late rhythm: start soon after the museums open when it is cooler and quieter, retreat indoors to an air-conditioned gallery or a shaded lunch during the fierce midday hours, and come back out in the late afternoon and long, balmy evening, when the temperature drops, the piazzas come alive and the floodlit monuments look their best. Stay hydrated, wear sun protection and a hat, and choose accommodation with reliable air-conditioning, which is essential rather than optional here. Note that August, particularly around Ferragosto (15 August), is when many Florentines leave the city for the coast or the hills, so some restaurants and shops close, though the main attractions stay open. On the plus side, summer brings the outdoor culture season — open-air cinema, concerts and the atmospheric evening life along the river — and the long light is lovely for photography. If you can choose your timing, spring or autumn is far more comfortable, but with the right rhythm and plenty of water and gelato, a summer visit is very doable.",
