    fullDescription: "The Scoppio del Carro, the 'Explosion of the Cart', is one of the oldest and most spectacular folk traditions in Florence, staged every Easter Sunday morning in the Piazza del Duomo and dating back over 350 years, with roots reaching to the era of the First Crusade. The centrepiece is the Brindellone, an enormous, elaborately decorated antique wooden cart, several storeys tall, which is hauled through the streets by a team of white oxen in a costumed historical procession and positioned between the Baptistery and the cathedral. During the Easter Mass, at the singing of the Gloria, the Archbishop lights a rocket shaped like a dove — the colombina — which represents the Holy Spirit and is sent whizzing along a wire strung from the high altar inside the cathedral, out through the great doors, to strike the cart and ignite it. What follows is a lengthy, deafening and dazzling display of fireworks, sparks and smoke that erupts from the cart to the delight of the huge crowd packed into the square. Tradition holds that a successful, complete explosion — with the dove flying smoothly back to the altar — augurs a good harvest and good fortune for the city and its people in the year ahead, a link to Florence's agricultural past. It is a free, family-friendly and utterly distinctive spectacle that blends religion, history, pageantry and pyrotechnics in a way found nowhere else. Because it draws large crowds to the piazza, it is well worth arriving early to secure a good vantage point, and combining it with the wider Easter celebrations makes for a memorable time to be in the city.",
