    fullDescription: "The Christmas season in Florence is atmospheric, traditional and beautiful, stretching from Advent through to Epiphany on 6 January, and while it brings more visitors and higher prices than the deep winter months either side of it, the city looks wonderful and the festivities are a delight. Florence dresses up with elegant festive lights strung along the main streets and squares, a large Christmas tree and a nativity scene in front of the Duomo, and the beloved Italian tradition of presepi — nativity scenes, from tiny hand-crafted tableaux to elaborate displays — set up in churches across the city. A highlight for many is the Christmas market: Piazza Santa Croce traditionally hosts a large German-style Weihnachtsmarkt, with wooden chalets selling crafts, decorations, gifts, mulled wine and festive food, bringing a cheerful northern-European flavour to the Renaissance square, and there are other markets and stalls around the centre. The season also offers seasonal concerts, and the shops, decked out for the holidays, make it a popular time for gift shopping along Via de' Tornabuoni and the Corso. The festivities climax with Epiphany on 6 January, celebrating the arrival of the Three Kings, which in Florence is marked by the spectacular Cavalcade of the Magi (Cavalcata dei Magi), a grand historical procession in Renaissance costume — recalling the famous Medici-era processions and the Benozzo Gozzoli frescoes in the Palazzo Medici — that winds through the centre from the Palazzo Pitti to the Duomo, with the three kings, pages, musicians and flag-throwers in full pageantry. Expect the holiday fortnight to be busier and pricier than the rest of winter, but the combination of lights, markets, nativity traditions and the magnificent Epiphany procession makes a Christmas-season visit to Florence genuinely magical.",
